# tgloggerd web

A server-rendered web interface for browsing the tgloggerd database:
users, groups, messages, files, admins, and the change history the daemon
records. It is a separate application from the logger daemon and is
read-only over the logger's data.

# Architecture

The web app is a standalone binary built from this directory. It does NOT
reuse the daemon's src/mysql/ layer: that layer is blocking JDBC and would
stall an event loop. Instead the web app is built on Drogon and talks to
MySQL through Drogon's asynchronous DbClient (coroutines), so a request
handler never blocks a loop thread.

Two databases, two users, two clients enforce a read/write split:

  - "ro"  -> user web_ro with GRANT SELECT ON tgloggerd.*, used for all
            browsing. It cannot modify the logger's data.
  - "app" -> user web_app with read/write on a separate tgloggerd_web
            schema that owns the web-only tables (web_users, web_audit).

Keeping the accounts in their own schema makes the read-only boundary a
database-level invariant, not just application convention.

Output escaping is a data-layer invariant. The template engine (inja) does
NOT auto-escape, so every attacker-controlled string (message text, names,
titles, bios, file names, ...) is passed through Render::esc() when the
data-access layer builds the template context. Templates only ever
interpolate already-escaped values.

# Layout

    CMakeLists.txt        Standalone project; C++20; builds Drogon.
    migrations/           golang-migrate SQL for the web-only schema.
    src/
      main.cpp            Config, sessions, DbClients, listener, --seed-admin.
      Config.hpp          Environment parsing.
      auth/               Password hashing, sessions, CSRF, filters, throttle.
      dao/                Async data access (Browse over "ro"; Accounts,
                          Audit over "app").
      views/              Render + esc(); templates/ holds the inja views.
      controllers/        Auth, Dashboard, Users, Groups, Messages, Media.
    static/               style.css and other static assets.
    third_party/          Vendored header-only inja and nlohmann/json.

# Build and run

The web app is built and run by Docker together with the daemon; see the
top-level README ("Quick start"). It is the "web" service in
docker-compose.yml and shares the toolchain image with the daemon. On
`docker compose up` the container compiles this directory (via `run.sh
web`, into web/build/), applies the web migrations, and starts the
server on http://localhost:8080.

MariaDB Connector/C — which Drogon's async MySQL client is built on — is
vendored as the submodule submodules/mariadb-connector-c and built in-tree
(cmake/FindMySQL.cmake points Drogon at it). This avoids the system
libmariadb-dev, which conflicts with the libmysqlclient-dev the daemon
needs, so both build in one image. `run.sh` initializes this submodule
(and Drogon) on first start.

# Database and accounts

The web schema (tgloggerd_web) and its two restricted users (web_ro,
web_app) are created automatically on MySQL's first start, from the .env
credentials, by docker/mysql/init/10-web-users.sh. There is no manual SQL
step. The web migrations, which create the web-only tables, are applied by
the container on every start (web_app owns tgloggerd_web, so it can run
them; at run time it performs only DML).

Change the WEB_DB_RO_PASSWORD / WEB_DB_APP_PASSWORD values in .env before
the first `docker compose up -d mysql`; they are baked in when the data
directory is initialized and are not re-applied on later starts.

# First administrator

Create (or reset) the first admin — it prompts for the password twice:

    docker compose exec web web/build/tgloggerd_web --seed-admin admin

Re-running --seed-admin for an existing user resets its password and
re-enables the account.

# Environment variables

All configuration is in .env (see .env.example). The web variables:

    WEB_LISTEN_ADDR        Bind address (0.0.0.0 in the container).
    WEB_LISTEN_PORT        Bind port (default 8080).
    WEB_THREADS            Event-loop threads (default 4).
    WEB_DB_HOST/PORT       MySQL server; fall back to TG_DB_HOST/PORT.
    WEB_DB_RO_NAME         Logger database (default tgloggerd).
    WEB_DB_RO_USER         Read-only user (default web_ro).
    WEB_DB_RO_PASSWORD     Read-only user password.
    WEB_DB_RO_CONNS        Read-only pool size (default 4).
    WEB_DB_APP_NAME        Web database (default tgloggerd_web).
    WEB_DB_APP_USER        Read-write user (default web_app).
    WEB_DB_APP_PASSWORD    Read-write user password.
    WEB_DB_APP_CONNS       Read-write pool size (default 2).
    WEB_SESSION_TIMEOUT    Session idle timeout in seconds (default 43200;
                           0 never expires).
    WEB_SECURE_COOKIE      1 sets Secure on the session cookie (default 1);
                           set 0 only for plain-HTTP local testing.
    WEB_LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS Failed logins per (ip, username) before throttling
                           (default 5).
    WEB_LOGIN_WINDOW       Throttle window in seconds (default 900).
    WEB_TEMPLATE_DIR       inja templates (default web/views/templates).
    WEB_STATIC_DIR         Static assets served at / (default web/static).
    WEB_STORAGE_DIR        File store; MUST equal the daemon's TG_STORAGE_DIR.

The container serves /media from the daemon's downloaded files (the shared
data/storage/files tree), so WEB_STORAGE_DIR must equal TG_STORAGE_DIR. The
example serves plain HTTP with WEB_SECURE_COOKIE=0; in production put a
TLS-terminating reverse proxy in front (see below) and set
WEB_SECURE_COOKIE=1.

# Deploying behind nginx (TLS)

The app serves plain HTTP. Terminate TLS in a reverse proxy and keep
WEB_SECURE_COOKIE=1 so the session cookie is only sent over HTTPS. A
minimal nginx server block:

    server {
        listen 443 ssl http2;
        server_name tgloggerd.example.org;

        ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/example/fullchain.pem;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/example/privkey.pem;
        add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000" always;

        location / {
            proxy_pass         http://127.0.0.1:8080;
            proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
            proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        }
    }

    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name tgloggerd.example.org;
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }

# Security notes

  - Authentication is app-level: argon2id password hashes (libsodium
    crypto_pwhash_str) and server-side sessions with a Secure, HttpOnly,
    SameSite=Lax cookie.
  - Every state-changing POST carries a per-session CSRF token, checked in
    constant time.
  - Login failures are throttled per (ip, username); login, logout and
    media access are written to web_audit.
  - All SQL uses bound parameters; pagination and cursor values are clamped
    bound integers.
  - Media is served from a path derived entirely from the database (the
    verified SHA-256 hex plus a sanitized extension), never from request
    input.

# Known limitations

  - No global message text search: the message text column is unindexed
    MEDIUMTEXT, so only bounded, chat-scoped listing is offered.
  - Range/seek support in newFileResponse is limited, so seeking within
    large media files may fall back to a full transfer.
